  "strategy": "We are to search for a sequence of nonnegative numbers that minimizes the evaluation function:\n\nscore = (2 * n * max_b) / (sum_a)^2\n\nwhere:\n\n  n = length of the sequence\n  b_sequence = np.convolve(sequence, mode='full')\n  max_b = maximum value in b_sequence\n  sum_a = sum of the sequence\n\nThe previous program found 1.505006 for n=768. We need a score lower than 2 and ideally around 1.5027.\n\nWe will use an approach based on the advantage of asymmetric step functions. The rationale is that asymmetric functions can avoid creating large convolution overlaps by concentrating high-value coefficients at one end of the sequence.\n\nWe will create candidate asymmetric initial sequences by:\n\n  1. Choosing a random breakpoint and splitting the sequence into two asymmetric parts.\n  2. Using a combination of exponential decay, logarithmic growth, and sine-modulation to ensure asymmetry.\n\nThen, we will use optimization methods (like the provided SMD and LP-FW) to refine these candidates. We will also explore sequences of different lengths (512, 640, 896, 1024, 1400) and use interpolation to build larger sequences.\n\nWe are not allowed to have computation time exceed 1800 seconds, so we'll use adaptive parameter settings and early termination.\n\nImplementation details:\n\n  - We'll use numpy and scipy.optimize for computations and optimization.\n  - We'll ensure the sum of any candidate is at least 0.01 by scaling if needed.\n  - We'll only consider nonnegative coefficients.\n  - We'll use the Fast Fourier Transform (FFT) method for convolution to speed up evaluations.\n\nSteps:\n\n1. **Initialization with Asymmetric Variants**:\n\n   Create multiple initial candidates using different asymmetric patterns (not just the given 9 variants). For example:\n\n    a. Exponential decay from the beginning with a delay.\n    b. Logarithmic growth at the beginning and exponential decay afterwards.\n    c. Combined sine and exponential modulation.\n\n   We'll sample these for lengths 512, 1024, and 1400.\n\n2. **Optimization using Smoothed Mirror Descent and LP-Frank-Wolf**:\n\n   We'll apply these optimization methods to our initial candidates. We use an adaptive learning rate schedule and allow the variants to change over iterations.\n\n3. **Peak-Targeted Optimization**:\n\n   Isolate the worst peak in the convolution and adjust coefficients surrounding that peak, using gradient descent.\n\n4. **Coordinate Descent**:\n\n   We'll also try coordinate descent to optimize each coefficient independently.\n\n   We'll carefully schedule the time for each optimization stage. If we get stuck in a local minimum, we'll use restart strategies with random noise to escape.\n\n5. **Interpolation and extrapolation**:\n\n   We'll interpolate between found optimal sequences and explore new lengths.\n\n   We'll return the best candidate found within the 1800 seconds.\n\nThe following is a detailed implementation of the plan.",
